(a) The city manager, or designee, may deny a city license under this chapter on any of the following grounds:
(1) Failure to meet state requirements for state license;
(2) Failure to pay required fees and taxes;
(3) Failure to provide valid information, documents, etc., as required by this chapter;
(4) False information in the application or attached documents;
(5) Failure to pass review by the chief of police;
(6) Failure to meet any and all requirements of chapter 10, article II of this Code;
(7) Improper residency of applicant, owner, or registered agents;
(8) Where there is evidence that the location or type of structure could create difficulty in police supervision;
(9) Where there is evidence that a license for the location would be detrimental to the property values in the area;
(10) Where there is evidence that the conduction of the business creates a disturbance, congregation of intoxicated persons, congregation of minors, allows minors to purchase, drink or possess alcoholic beverages on the premises, or causes the police to answer complaints or make extra surveillance of the premises;
(11) Failure to comply with any other ordinance of the city;
(12) Failure to post and maintain proper signs and advertisements required in this chapter;
(13) Failure to meet distance or location requirements;
(14) Prior convictions as provided in this article;
(15) Failure to meet any other requirements of this chapter for a license of the class applied for, or any other requirement in any other provision of this Code or the city Charter; or
(16) Failure to meet all city zoning requirements.
(b) If an applicant for a license under this chapter is denied by the city manager, or designee, for the reasons set forth in subsections (a)(1)—(11) of this section, the applicant may not reapply for a license under this chapter for a period of 12 months. If an applicant for a license under this chapter is denied by the city manager, or designee, for the reasons set forth in subsections (a)(12)—(16) of this section, the applicant may reapply at the discretion of the mayor and board of aldermen.
(c) Otherwise, the city manager, or designee, may issue any city license provided for in this chapter.
(d) Consideration of a license application by the city manager, or designee, shall be in accordance with the provisions of this chapter.
(Ord. No. 2000-3, 3-2-2000; Ord. No. 2021-15, § 1, 6-17-2021)

252:
(a) The city manager, or designee, or his/her designee shall be authorized to suspend or revoke an alcoholic license in accordance with the procedures set forth below. In the event the city manager, or designee, or his/her designee seeks to suspend or revoke an occupation tax certificate, the city manager, or designee, or his/her designee shall give written notification to the applicant of such action and such notice shall contain a specification of the violation or violations and shall be served upon the licensee at least five days prior to the hearing. The applicant shall be given written notice of the time and place of the hearing.
(b) The city manager, or designee, or his/her designee shall be authorized to suspend or revoke an alcoholic license in the event of any one or more of the following:
(1) An applicant gave false or misleading information in the original or renewal application process;
(2) An applicant has knowingly allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on the premises and/or knowingly allowed possession, use or sale of controlled substances to a minor on the premises;
(3) An applicant has knowingly allowed the violation of an ordinance of the city or a violation of any criminal law of the state (a misdemeanor or a felony) to occur on the premises; and that such violation is materially related to the operation of said business;
(4) An applicant (or licensee) has been convicted of any drug-related, alcohol-related or sex-related crime by the state or the city regarding an offense which was committed on the premises or which would otherwise violate the provisions of this chapter;
(5) An applicant fails to pay any fee, occupation tax, fine or other amount of money due to the city under this chapter or any other taxing ordinance of the city;
(6) An applicant or the owner alters or allows to be altered; the alcohol license or the applicant, or the owner changes the information, defaces, destroys, misuses, abuses, or improperly alters or misrepresents the alcohol license certificate.
(c) In the event the city manager, or designee, shall suspend or revoke any alcohol license, the suspension or revocation shall be for a period of not less than one day, nor more than 365 days, within the discretion of the city manager, or designee. Any suspension or revocation shall be sent by the city by certified mail, return receipt requested. No applicant may apply for an alcohol license during any period of suspension or revocation. In any hearing conducted, the city manager, or designee, shall consider, among other things, the severity of the allegations, the evidence submitted, and the testimony presented, in making any decision on suspension or revocation and the duration of either.
(d) An applicant or any interested person may appeal an administrative action or decision of the city manager, or designee. An appeal must be made to the mayor and board of aldermen. All appeals shall be submitted in writing, shall state reasons for the appeal, and shall be filed with the city clerk within ten days of the decision. The board shall give at least five-days' notice before hearing the appeal. Such notice shall be given to the general public and parties of interest. The mayor and board of aldermen may affirm, reverse, or modify the decision of the city manager, or designee.
(e) No licensee may apply for or receive a new or renewal license during any period of suspension/revocation hereunder.
(Ord. No. 2008-17, 6-19-2008; Ord. No. 2013-11, § 5, 4-18-2013; Ord. No. 2013-20, § 3, 5-16-2013; Ord. No. 2017-22, 12-21-2017; Ord. No. 2019-31, § 1, 11-7-2019; Ord. No. 2021-15, § 1, 6-17-2021)
